## Recommendation

Compared with round 4, r5 changes the white page and section headings to purple; they have less brightness but remain clearly readable and distinguishable from pink categories and cyan identifiers.
Across both 1280 sidebar states, 640 and 390, I see no loss of Officina information or deterioration in wrapping: work identifiers, titles, next-mover notes, counts and the absent-budget explanation remain available.
The captures still show closed Health disclosures and no populated Budget; at 390, Health details fall below the captured viewport, so those states cannot be judged from these images.
Round 6’s pink-disclosure advisory does not warrant a colour change: “Lex status” and “Process health” read as category labels, their arrows identify disclosure controls, and adjacent shapes and severity words carry status independently.
Restricting pink to inert labels would introduce an unnecessary exception; cyan underlined links denote navigation without requiring every interactive control to share their colour, although these closed captures do not show the expanded link adjacency.
Officina reads as one system with the supplied console reference through purple headings, pink categories, cyan identifiers and links, neutral supporting text and shared surfaces; a direct claim about the current Home screen is not established.
Verdict: passed